#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203858 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204085 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "efe27056-c688-56de-9859-98464f57ef64" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Basic Subset Gradients"}] :question/id #uuid "aba1787c-af4b-5adc-8302-6302e891978b" :question/math-academy-id "e-17532" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y,z,w) = xyzw$, compute $\\nabla_{x,w} \\: f$." :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\nabla_{x,w} \\: f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to $x$ and $w$ only.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} = \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyzw) &= yzw \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂w} = \\frac{∂}{∂w}(xyzw) &= xyz\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇_{x, w}f &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂w}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}yzw \\\\ xyz\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203859 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204086 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6646d65-7c0f-56ae-995d-1de831893ab9" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Parameter Subset Gradients"}] :question/id #uuid "9599c265-6015-5ad4-b379-d72a67a1a475" :question/math-academy-id "e-17533" :question/problem "Given the quadratic function $f(x,y; a,b) = a - bx^{2}+y^3$, compute $\\nabla_{a,b} \\: f$." :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\nabla_{a,b} \\: f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to $a$ and $b$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂a} &= \\frac{∂}{∂a}(a - bx^{2} + y^{3}) = 1 \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂b} &= \\frac{∂}{∂b}(a - bx^{2} + y^{3}) =-x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇_{a, b}f &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂a} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂b}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -x^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203860 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204088 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26c6e982-dcf3-5521-84d9-5d8ca8b51a33" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Second-Order Derivatives"}] :question/id #uuid "1639c64c-655b-5822-8464-0f4a704887df" :question/math-academy-id "e-17534" :question/problem "Given the function $f(\\mathbf{x}; \\mathbf{w}) = (w_1x_1 - w_2+x_2)^2$, compute $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{w}}(\\nabla_{\\mathbf{w}} \\: f)$." :question/worked-solution "First, we compute $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{w}} \\: f$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂w_{1}} &= \\frac{∂}{∂w_{1}}(w_{1}x_{1} - w_{2} + x_{2})^{2} = 2x_{1}(w_{1}x_{1} - w_{2} + x_{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂w_{2}} &= \\frac{∂}{∂w_{2}}(w_{1}x_{1} - w_{2} + x_{2})^{2} =-2(w_{1}x_{1} - w_{2} + x_{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂w_{1}^{2}} = 2x_{1}^{2} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂w_{1}∂w_{2}} =-2x_{1} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂w_{2}∂w_{1}} =-2x_{1} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂w_{2}^{2}} = 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇_{w}(∇_{w}f) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂w_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂w_{1}∂w_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂w_{2}∂w_{1}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂w_{2}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2x_{1}^{2} & -2x_{1} \\\\ -2x_{1} & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203863 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204087 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64331ffc-80a5-50c1-a7ac-d346458ce997" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Vector Subset Gradients"}] :question/id #uuid "91b597c2-12a1-5c93-93fb-dd9dd4a3a9fc" :question/math-academy-id "e-17704" :question/problem "Given the function\n$f(\\mathbf{x}; \\mathbf{w}) = w_1x_1^{3} + w_2x_2^{3}$,\ncompute $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{w}} \\: f$ with the input $[3,-2]$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{w}} \\: f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to each component of $\\mathbf{w}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂w_{1}} &= \\frac{∂}{∂w_{1}}(w_{1}x_{1}^{3} + w_{2}x_{2}^{3}) = x_{1}^{3} \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂w_{2}} &= \\frac{∂}{∂w_{2}}(w_{1}x_{1}^{3} + w_{2}x_{2}^{3}) = x_{2}^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, with $[3,-2]$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇_{w}f &= \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}^{3} \\\\ x_{2}^{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}27 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204559 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204085 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "efe27056-c688-56de-9859-98464f57ef64" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Basic Subset Gradients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "be30e805-f1c6-5479-9f43-1b97f941cb88" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203321} {:answer-field/id #uuid "e3f1f724-9806-58ef-aa98-ba6847cbc23c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203322}] :question/id #uuid "90dbed3e-86ce-5b42-be6c-68de8714331b" :question/math-academy-id "q-263486" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x, y, z) = x^{2}y + yz^{2}$, compute $∇_{x, y}f$.\n\n| $∇_{x, y}f =$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204563 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204088 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26c6e982-dcf3-5521-84d9-5d8ca8b51a33" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Second-Order Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "32c25c87-9230-542f-9e1e-4e04d4a253ab" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203331} {:answer-field/id #uuid "7c6796e7-71f5-5e98-9a2e-b52f1df475d0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203332}] :question/id #uuid "42c18789-1e5f-52ac-ac15-9c282210e0f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-263490